  "statement": "(i) Write down the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{1/2}$. (ii) Hence find the expansion of $\\sqrt{1-x}$ up to the term in $x^{3}$. (iii) Use it to estimate $\\sqrt{0.98}$.",
  "solution": "(i) $(1+x)^{1/2}=1+\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12(\\tfrac12-1)\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+\\tfrac12(\\tfrac12-1)(\\tfrac12-2)\\frac{x^{3}}{6}=1+\\tfrac12 x-\\tfrac18 x^{2}+\\tfrac1{16}x^{3}$. (ii) Replace $x$ by $-x$: $\\sqrt{1-x}=1-\\tfrac12 x-\\tfrac18 x^{2}-\\tfrac1{16}x^{3}$. (iii) $\\sqrt{0.98}=\\sqrt{1-0.02}\\approx 1-0.01-0.00005-0.000000125=0.989949875$.",
